## Cell surface staining
Cells were blocked in \(\mathrm{PBS}/2\%\) FBS, on ice for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\). \(2\;\times\;10^{5}\) cells/well were transferred to a 96-well V-bottomed plate and pelleted at \(300\;\mathrm{xg}\), \(4\;\mathrm{min}\), \(4\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The supernatant was removed by inverting the plate. The pellet was resuspended with \(25\;\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of diluted, conjugated antibody and incubated in the dark, on ice for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\). The cells were then pelleted at \(300\;\mathrm{xg}\), \(4\;\mathrm{min}\), \(4\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and washed twice with \(75\;\upmu\mathrm{l}\) PBS. The final pellet was resuspended in \(100\;\upmu\mathrm{l}\) PBS; \(100\;\upmu\mathrm{l}\) SYTOX Blue Nucleic Acid Stain \((5\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\), ThermoFisher #S11348) was added immediately prior to flow cytometer analysis to allow for live/dead cell identification. Antibodies used were CD14 (Biorad, #MCA1218F, 1:50) with isotype control (Sigma, #SAB4700700), CD16 (Biorad, #MCA9271PE, 1:200) with isotype control (Biorad, #MCA928PE), CD163 (Biorad, #MCA2311F, 1:100) with isotype control (Sigma, #F6397), CD169 (Biorad, #MCA2316F, 1:100) with isotype control (Sigma, #F6397) and CD172a (Southern Biotech, #4525-09, 1:400) with isotype control (Biorad, #MCA928PE).
## Toxoplasma infection and staining
PAMs and PSCdMs were plated \(48\;\mathrm{h}\) prior to infection at \(8\;\times\;10^{5}/\) well in a 12-well tissue culture plate in cRPMI medium. The cells were fed with cRPMI \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) before infection. The next day the medium was aspirated and the cells infected with Toxoplasma gondii at \(\mathrm{MOI} = 1\) in cRPMI for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) at \(37\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) post-infection the cells were collected using a cell scraper and pelleted at \(600\;\mathrm{xg}\) for \(4\;\mathrm{min}\) then washed in \(500\;\upmu\mathrm{l}\) PBS. One half was lysed for genomic DNA recovery to determine Toxoplasma DNA copies and the other half used to prepare RNA for RT-qPCR analysis.
## Salmonella enterica serovar Typhimurium infection and staining
PAMs and PSCdMs were plated \(48\;\mathrm{h}\) prior to infection at \(5\;\times\;10^{5}/\) well in a 12-well tissue culture plate in cRPMI. The day before infection, a single colony of Salmonella enterica serovar Typhimurium strain 4/47, expressing EGFP from plasmid pFVP25.1 [32], was cultured for \(16\;\mathrm{h}\) in \(3\;\mathrm{ml}\) LB medium \(+100\;\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}\) Ampicillin. The \(\mathrm{OD}_{600}\) absorbance was measured on a
spectrophotometer and used to determine the bacterial cell concentration using the online tool http://www. labtools.us/bacterial-cell-number-od600/. The cells were washed twice with PBS and infected with bacteria diluted in cRPMI medium at an \(\mathrm{MOI}\,=\,2\) for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\) at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . Following two washes with PBS, the cells were then treated with \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}\) gentamicin in cRPMI for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to kill extracellular bacteria. Surviving intracellular bacteria were harvested at \(0\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and \(3\,\mathrm{{h}}\) after gentamicin treatment by washing the cells twice with PBS then lysing with \(1\%\) TritonX100. Ten-fold serial dilutions were plated on to LB/Ampicillin culture plates and incubated overnight at \(37\,^{\circ}\!C.\) . Colonies were counted the next day. For staining, cells were fixed on glass coverslips after gentamicin treatment in \(4\%\) Formaldehyde for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\) then permeabilised in \(\mathrm{PBS}/0.1\%\) TritonX100 for \(10\,\mathrm{{min}}\) before staining with Phalloidin AF647 (1:1000) and DAPI (1:10,000) in PBS at room temperature for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\) in the dark. Cells were washed twice with PBS before mounting on glass slides and imaging on a Leica LSM10 confocal microscope.
## Escherichia coli infection
PAMs and PSCdMs were infected with Escherichia coli strain TOP10 at a \(\mathrm{MOI}=10\) using the same protocol as for Salmonella infection and surviving bacteria harvested at \(0\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) after gentamicin treatment.
## PRRSV infection and staining
PAMs and PSCdMs were plated on non-coated tissue culture plates in cRPMI at a density of \(1\,\times\,10^{5}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,24\,\mathrm{h}\) prior to infection. Where indicated macrophages were pre-treated with poly(I:C) \((25\,\upmu\mathrm{g/ml)}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{{h}}\) prior to infection. Cells were infected with PRRSV (SU1-Bel) at \(\mathrm{MOI}\,=\,1\) in cRPMI for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(37\,^{\circ}\!C.\) The inoculum was then removed, and the cells fed with fresh cRPMI. At 19 hpi cells were washed twice with PBS and detached using a cell scraper. Cells were fixed in \(4\%\) Formaldehyde for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\) then permeabilised with \(0.1\%\) TritonX100/PBS for \(10\,\mathrm{{min}}\) . After washing twice with PBS, the cells were blocked in \(5\%\mathrm{FBS/PBS}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\) prior to incubating with primary antibody (SDOW-17A, 1:5000) for \(45\,\mathrm{{min}}\) in blocking solution. Following two washes with PBS, the cells were then incubated with secondary antibody (Goat \(\upalpha\) -mouse AF488, 1:5000) for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) in the dark before staining with Phalloidin AF647 (1:1000) and DAPI (1:10,000) for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\) in the dark. After two washes with PBS, the cells were analysed by flow cytometry.
## ASFV infection and growth assays
Porcine monocyte macrophages (PMMs) were harvested from heparinised blood taken from pigs housed at the APHA under housing and sampling regulations, licence
Porcine monocyte macrophages (PMMs) were harvested from heparinised blood taken from pigs housed at the APHA under housing and sampling regulations, licence PP1962684, approved by the APHA Animal Welfare and Ethical Review Board and conducted in accordance with the Animals (Scientific procedures) Act UK. Blood was centrifuged and plasma, leukocytes (buffy coat) and erythrocyte fractions harvested. The leukocytes were washed in PBS, followed by two washes with BD Pharm Lyse (#555899). After two further washes in PBS, cells were re-suspended in RPMI supplemented with \(20\%\) v/v autologous plasma, harvested from the initial centrifugation step, and \(100\,\mathrm{U/ml}\) Penicillin-Streptomycin (Gibco, #15140122). Cells were incubated in 96-well plates at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) for \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) prior to infection with ASFV. Infections with ASFV were performed at the APHA in biosecure containment laboratories licenced for handling of level 4 specified animal pathogens. ASFV strain Armenia 07 diluted in RPMI was added to PSCdMs, PAMs and PMMs at an MOI of 1 in 96-well plates. After \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) incubation at \(37\,^{\circ}\!C,\) the virus inoculum was removed and, for quantification of viral replication by qPCR, was replaced with \(200\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of Macrophage Maturation media. For observation of ASFV infection by detection of haemadsorbance additional wells were set up in which the virus inoculum was removed and replaced with \(200\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) Macrophage Maturation medium supplemented with \(1\%\) v/v porcine erythrocytes and \(1\%\) porcine plasma. Plates were incubated at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) for up to 5 days. Formation of HAD rosettes due to haemadsorbance of erythrocytes to infected macrophages was observed by light microscopy. To quantify ASFV replication and release into the supernatant \(140\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of media was removed from wells after 0, 24 and \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) and nucleic acid extracted using Qiamp viral RNA mini extraction kit (Qiagen, #52904). Viral DNA levels were quantified by qPCR using primers and probe that detect the ASFV VP72 gene [53] with the Quantifast Pathogen PCR kit (Qiagen) and the following cycle conditions: \(1\,\times\,95\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{{min}}\) followed by 50 cycles of \(95\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(15\,\mathrm{s},\) \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(1\,\mathrm{min}\) The copies of viral genome were determined by comparison Cq values to those of a standard comprised of a dilution series of the plasmid pASFV-VP72 encoding a fragment of the VP72 gene.
ASFV infection with strain Benin 97/1 was performed at the Pirbright Institute essentially as described previously [54]. ASFV infection was monitored by formation of HAD rosettes, and immunocytochemical detection of ASFV VP72 expression [55]. ASFV replication was measured by \(\mathrm{TCID}_{50}\) assay and calculated using the Spearman-Karber method.
## Gene editing REX1-EGFP knock-in
The pig REX1 targeting vector was constructed in two stages. First, the homology arms were amplified from porcine PSC genomic DNA using primers with tails containing the inverted guide sequence (5'HA+Guide_
Forward:CTTCTTTCACTGATTTGTATTGGTTCAA GGAGAGCGCAAAACTA,3'HA+guide_Reverse:CTTC TTTCACTGATTTGTATTGGAGTTGATTCAAATGG ATTGACA). The PCR product was then TA-cloned into the pCR4-TOPO TA vector backbone (ThermoFisher #450071) and linearised by inverse PCR using primers positioned either side of, and designed to exclude, the Rex1 STOP codon (HA3_inv_For AAGAAGACTGAA AATAATCC, HA3_inv_Reverse:CTGATTTGTATTGG CCTTTG). In addition, a T2A-EGFP-IRES-PURObGHpA cassette was amplified by PCR using primers with 15 bp tails homologous to the sequence either side of the Rex1 STOP codon (T2ARex1_Forward:GCGAAT ACAAATCAGGGCTCCGGAGAGGGCAGAG, bGHpa Rex1_Reverse:ATTTTCAGTCTTCTTCCATAGAGCCC ACCGCATCC). Second, the linearised homology arms and amplified reporter/selection cassette were assembled by Gibson assembly (NEB, #E2621S) and individual clones were sequence verified. A CRISPR/Cas9 guide sequence was identified using Benchling (www.benchling. com) that generates a double-strand break 8 bp upstream of the pig \( REX1 \) STOP codon (Rex1_363 CTTCTTTCACTGATTTGTAT). The sgRNA was synthesised by Synthego. For editing, \( 7.5\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) sgRNA \( (100\,\upmu\mathrm{M}) \) was combined with \( 5\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) Cas9 protein \( (20\,\upmu\mathrm{M}, \) Synthego) at room temperature for \( 10\,\mathrm{{min}} \) to form ribonucleoprotein complexes (RNPs), then \( 1\,\upmu\mathrm{g} \) targeting vector was added to the RNPs and made up to \( 30\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) with P3 Primary Cell Solution \( (82\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) Nucleofector Solution \( +\,18\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) Supplement per \( 100\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) ) and kept on ice prior to transfection. Porcine PSCs were passaged as normal and \( 5\times10^{5} \) cells were resuspended in \( 70\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) of Amaxa P3 Primary Cell Solution. The RNP complex was mixed with the cells, transferred to a transfection cuvette then nucleofected on an Amaxa 4D Nucleofector using program CG-104. The cells were resuspended in pEPSCM \( ^+ \) ROCKi and plated over two wells of a 6-well plate containing mitotically-inactivated STO feeder cells. Medium was changed the next day for pEPSCM without ROCKi. Seventy-two hours post-transfection, the cells were passaged and plated at \( 2\,\times\,10^{4}/\mathrm{cm}^{2} \). Puromycin selection \( (0.2\,\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}) \) was added \( 24\,\mathrm{{h}} \) later. After 10 days, six colonies were picked and passaged as normal into a 96-well tissue culture plate. Clones were expanded and screened by PCR for evidence of editing. Correctly targeted clones were identified at both the \( 5^{\,\prime} \) an \( 3^{\,\prime} \) ends of the integration site by PCR amplification of genomic DNA using \( 5^{\,\prime} \) primers (xF1 - GTTTTCTGAGTACGTGCCAGGC, iR1 - CGGGTCTTGTAGTTGCCGTCGT) and \( 3^{\,\prime} \) primers (iF2 - TGGGAAGACAATAGCAGGCATG, xR3 – CA CACCCCCGCCAAACTGCTG) under the following cycle conditions: \( -\ 98\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 1\,\mathrm{min} \) then 32 cycles of \( 98\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 10\,\mathrm{s} \), \( 69\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 30\,\mathrm{s} \) and \( 72\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 1\,\mathrm{min} \) followed by a final extension of \( 72\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 10\,\mathrm{{min}} \). For each screen, one
primer was located outside the homology arm sequence and the other within the reporter/selection cassette. Targeted fragments of \( 885\,\mathrm{bp} \) and \( 1213\,\mathrm{bp} \) were expected for the \( 5^{\,\prime} \) and \( 3^{\,\prime} \) screens respectively.
## Gene editing IRF3 deletion
A pair of CRISPR/Cas9 guide sequences was designed to delete the pig IRF3 coding sequence. Guide sequences were identified using Benchling (www.benchling.com) and synthesised by Synthego (IRF3_1094 CGAGGGCTT CTGAGTTCCCAT, IRF3_5441 ACATGGATTTCTAG GCCGCT). For editing, \( 3.75\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) of each sgRNA \( (100\,\mathrm{mM}) \) was combined with \( 5\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) Cas9 protein \( (20\,\mathrm{mM}, \) Synthego) at room temperature for \( 10\,\mathrm{{min}} \) to form RNPs then \( 17.5\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) P3 Primary Cell Solution added and the RNPs kept on ice prior to transfection. Porcine PSCs were passaged as normal and \( 5\,\times\,10^{5} \) cells were resuspended in \( 70\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) of Amaxa P3 Primary Cell Solution. The RNP complex was mixed with the cells, transferred to a transfection cuvette then nucleofected on an Amaxa 4D Nucleofector using program CG-104. The cells were resuspended in pEPSCM \( ^+ \) ROCKi and plated over two wells of a 6-well plate containing mitotically-inactivated STO feeder cells. Medium was changed the next day for pEPSCM without ROCKi. Seventy-two hours later, the cells were passaged and plated at low density \( (2.5\times10^{2} \), \( 1\,\times\,10^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}) \). After 9–11 days, 80 colonies were picked and passaged as normal into a 96-well tissue culture plate. Clones were expanded and screened by PCR for evidence of editing. Genomic DNA was PCR amplified using a pool of two forward primers (scrnF1 - AGGCCG TCTGTTTGGGAGGAA, Ex8F1 - TTGTCCCCATGT GTCTCCGG) and one reverse primer (scrnR1 - TGA-CAGACAGGACGTTTAGGCA) under the following cycle conditions: \( -\ 98\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 1\,\mathrm{min} \) then 32 cycles of \( 98\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 10\,\mathrm{s} \), \( 68\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 30\,\mathrm{s} \) and \( 72\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 1\,\mathrm{min} \) followed by a final extension of \( 72\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 10\,\mathrm{min} \). Two wild-type fragments of 5,311 bp and \( 640\,\mathrm{bp} \), and an edited fragment of 964 bp were expected although the 5,311 bp fragment failed to amplify under these conditions.
## Lentivirus packaging
HEK293T cells were grown to \( 80\% \) confluence in a T175 flask then transfected with \( 15\,\upmu\mathrm{g} \) lentiviral plasmid together with \( 12\,\upmu\mathrm{g} \) psPax2 and \( 3\,\upmu\mathrm{g} \) pVSV packaging plasmids using \( 15\,\upmu\mathrm{l} \) Lipofectamine 2000. The medium containing lentivirus was harvested at \( 24\,\mathrm{{h}} \) and \( 48\,\mathrm{h} \). The medium was stored at \( 4\,^{\circ}\!C \) until all harvests were collected then pooled and filtered through a \( 0.45\,\upmu\mathrm{m} \) filter. Filtered lentivirus was either stored in aliquots at \( -80\,^{\circ}\!C \) or further purified and concentrated using the Lenti-X Maxi Purification kit (Takara #631234) according to the manufacturer's instructions. Concentrated lentivirus was stored in aliquots at \( -80\,^{\circ}\!C \).
## Lentiviral transduction
Lentiviral transductionPorcine PSCdMs were plated in Macrophage Maturation Medium at \(3 \times 10^{5}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\). Seventy-two hours later, the medium was removed, and the cells were transduced with lentivirus \((250\,\upmu\mathrm{l}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) in Macrophage Maturation Medium containing \(2\,\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{ml}\) Polybrene (Santa Cruz, sc134220) by spinfection (centrifugation at \(1000\times g\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(32\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). Following spinfection, the medium was replaced with fresh Maturation Medium, and the cells incubated at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/5\,\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). The cells were imaged and analysed by flow cytometry 7–8 days post-transduction. For assessing transduction efficiency, a CMV-GFP-Puro-expressing lentivirus (Addgene #17448) was used at \(\mathrm{MOI}=1\). For editing of \(CD163\), a dual guide RNA lentivirus (Addgene #67974) was modified to express the \(CD163\) guides SL26 and SL28 [41] by cloning a gBlock containing the crRNASL26-tracrRNA-mU6-crRNASL28 sequence into the BbsI site. The \(CD163\) guide lentivirus was co-transduced along with the Cas9-expressing lentivirus, lenti-Cas9-Blast (Addgene #52962) at \(1{:}1\,\mathrm{v/v}\). The empty dual guide lentivirus was used as a negative control. CRISPR/Cas9-mediated deletion of \(CD163\) exon7 was determined by genomic DNA PCR amplification using primers (CD163scrnF - ACCTTGATGATTGTAC TCTT, CD163scrnR - TTGTCCCAGTGAGAGTTGCAG) under the following cycle conditions \(98\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{min}\) then 32 cycles of \(98\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{s}\), \(67\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{s}\) and \(72\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{min}\) followed by a final extension of \(72\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\). A wild-type fragment of 941 bp and an edited fragment of 454 bp were expected.
## RNASeq analysis
RNASeq analysisTotal RNA was prepared for four technical replicates of porcine PSC and PSCdM samples (K3 cell line) using Qiagen RNeasy kit (#74104) following the manufacturer's protocol including the recommended on-column DNase treatment. Short-read RNA-Seq libraries were prepared using TruSeq stranded mRNA Library Prep kit (Illumina). In short, poly-A containing mRNA molecules were purified and fragmented. The cleaved fragments were copied into cDNA using reverse transcriptase and random primers. The library was sequenced on an Illumina Novaseq platform to generate \(2 \times 100\,\mathrm{bp}\) paired-end reads.
